course: physics
lesson: 32
title: Fuels, the Sun and saving energy
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A log on a campfire holds energy the tree caught from sunlight; burning joins the wood with oxygen from the air and lets that energy out as heat and light.
explain: A fire needs three things, fuel, oxygen and heat, and taking away any one puts it out, which is how a fire blanket works: it keeps the oxygen away.
explain: Coal, oil and gas power stations burn fuel to boil water into steam that spins turbines, but these fossil fuels took millions of years to form, will run out, and put carbon dioxide into the air, which warms the planet.
explain: Wind, sunshine and flowing water keep coming back, so they are called renewable, and the cleanest energy of all is the energy you do not waste: lights off, LED bulbs on.
explain: Energy also hides in height: a sledge at the top of a hill whizzes down with no push, paid for by the hard walk up.
fact: Burning joins a fuel with oxygen from the air and releases its stored energy as heat and light.
fact: A fire needs fuel, oxygen and heat; take one away and it goes out, and a fire blanket works by keeping the oxygen out.
fact: Coal, oil and gas power stations burn fuel to heat water into steam, and the steam spins turbines that make electricity.
fact: Burning fossil fuels puts carbon dioxide into the air, which warms the planet.
fact: Fossil fuels took millions of years to form and are being used much faster, so one day they will run out.
fact: Wind, sunshine and flowing water keep coming back, so they are called renewable.
fact: A bike dynamo spins a magnet in a coil and turns the movement of the wheel into electricity for the lamp; the faster the wheel spins, the more it makes.
fact: A falling object turns the energy of its height into speed, so a glass dropped from higher up hits harder.
fact: An LED bulb gives the same light as an old bulb for a fraction of the electricity.
fact: A petrol engine wastes much of its fuel as heat, which is why a car needs a radiator; electric motors waste much less.
fact: Plants grow towards light because their leaves need it to make food, storing the energy as sugar.
fact: The Sun shines because tiny parts of hydrogen join together deep inside it, a process called fusion, and it will keep shining for about another five billion years.
fact: Walking or riding up a hill stores energy that turns back into speed on the way down.
exercise: What happens to wood when it burns? | It joins with oxygen and lets out stored energy as heat and light | It melts into a liquid and then disappears | It turns into pure energy with nothing left | It soaks up heat from the air around it
exercise: Which three things does a fire need? | Fuel, oxygen and heat | Wood, water and wind | Smoke, ash and sparks | Paper, coal and matches
exercise: How does a fire blanket put out a fire? | It keeps the oxygen out | It cools the fire with water inside | It blows the flames away | It soaks up the fuel
exercise: How does a coal power station make electricity? | Burning coal boils water into steam that spins turbines | Coal is squeezed until sparks fly out | Coal dust is mixed with water to make an electric current flow | The smoke turns a fan in the chimney
exercise: Why are people trying to use less coal and oil? | Burning them puts carbon dioxide in the air and warms the planet | Coal and oil make the electricity too strong | They are too heavy to carry to power stations | They only work in the winter
exercise: Why will fossil fuels one day run out? | They took millions of years to form and are used much faster | They evaporate into the air every summer | Scientists have decided to stop making any more of them in factories | They turn into water after a while
exercise: What does renewable mean? | It keeps coming back, like wind, sunshine and flowing water | It is made in a factory every day | It can only be used once | It is brand new and never used
exercise: How can pedalling a bike light its lamp? | A dynamo spins a magnet in a coil and makes electricity | The lamp is lit by the heat coming off your legs as you pedal | The chain rubs and makes sparks | The wind blows into the lamp
exercise: Why does a glass break more easily when dropped from higher up? | It has more energy from its height, so it hits harder | It gets heavier as it falls further | The air makes the glass brittle | Glass breaks only when it falls above a certain height
exercise: Why are LED bulbs better than old bulbs? | They give the same light for much less electricity | They are much brighter, but they use more electricity | They never need electricity at all | They make the room warmer
exercise: Why does a car need a radiator? | Its engine turns much of the fuel into heat | The radio inside needs cooling down all the time | The tyres get hot on the road and need cooling | The seats must be kept warm for the passengers
exercise: Why do plants turn towards the light? | Their leaves need light to make food | They like to watch what is going on outside | They are pulled towards the window glass | They are trying to escape from the pot
exercise: What makes the Sun shine? | Hydrogen joining together deep inside, called fusion | Coal and wood burning away in huge piles on its surface | Light it borrows from other stars | Lightning flashing inside it
exercise: Where does a sledge's speed come from as it whizzes down a hill? | Energy stored by climbing up the hill | The snow pushing it along from behind | The cold air pulling it down the slope | The sledge's own little engine hidden inside
exercise: Which of these wastes the least energy as heat? | An electric motor | A petrol engine | An old glowing-wire bulb | A coal fire
test: dad's like "turn the lights off, we're not made of money". why tho? | Making electricity costs money and often burns fuel | Lights only work for a few hours each day, then break | Leaving lights on makes them brighter | Turned-off bulbs charge themselves up
test: A pan of oil catches fire on the cooker. Why would putting a lid or fire blanket over it help? | It cuts off the oxygen the fire needs | It squashes the flames flat against the oil | It turns the burning oil into plain water | It cools the hot pan down straight away
test: Mia says the Sun burns coal like a giant fireplace. Put it right. | It shines by fusion, hydrogen joining together deep inside | It burns wood from old forests | It is lit by lightning every morning | It reflects light from the Moon
test: What would happen if everyone kept using oil much faster than it forms? | It would eventually run out | It would grow back each spring | Nothing, oil never runs out | It would turn into coal
test: A town wants energy that will never run out. Which should it choose? | Wind and solar power | More coal power stations | Burning old car tyres | Bigger oil tanks
test: Why does your little cousin's houseplant lean towards the window? | Its leaves are stretching for the light they need | It wants to see the garden | It is being blown by a draught | Plants always lean over towards any glass and windows
test: shut up and pedal, the lamp's only bright when we're going fast, whyyy | The dynamo makes more electricity when the wheel spins faster | The lamp gets scared when you stop | The battery only charges when you are fast | It's the wind that lights it
test: In a cartoon, a sledge rolls up a hill all by itself with nobody pushing. What's wrong? | Going up a hill needs energy from something, like a push or a motor | Nothing, sledges love going uphill | Sledges can only go sideways | Snow pushes things uphill
test: After a long drive the bonnet of the car is too hot to touch. Why? | The engine turned much of its fuel into heat | Sunlight was shining down on it all the way home | The tyres rubbed against the bonnet and made it hot | The radio inside the car heats the whole bonnet up
test: The Sun has shone for billions of years. Will it go out tomorrow? | No, it has fuel for about another five billion years | Yes, it is nearly out of coal | Yes, it goes out every hundred years and lights up again | Nobody has any idea at all
test: Which loses more energy as wasted heat: a petrol car engine or an electric car's motor? | The petrol engine | The electric motor | Both lose exactly the same | Neither loses any energy as heat
exam: Identify the three requisites for the sustenance of combustion. | Fuel, oxygen and heat | Water, wind and light | Smoke, ash and sparks | Electricity, metal and air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises renewable sources of energy? | They are replenished naturally and are not depleted by use | They are manufactured afresh in factories each day | They may be employed on a single occasion only | They are derived exclusively from the burning of coal and wood
exam: Ascertain the principal environmental objection to the combustion of fossil fuels. | It releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, thereby warming the planet | It renders the generated electricity excessively powerful | It depletes the oxygen supply of every household | It causes the oceans to freeze
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the efficiency of light-emitting diode lamps. | They yield equal illumination for a fraction of the electrical energy | They consume more energy but yield superior colour | They require no electrical supply whatsoever | They convert the greater part of their energy into heat
